      "problem": "If $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}$ are elements of $A,$ but $0,1$ and $2$ are not elements of $B,$ then which of the following is true?",
      "worked_solution": "If $a$ is an element of $S,$ we write $a\\in S.$ Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S,$ we write $a\\notin S.$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}$ are elements of $A,$ we write $-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\in A.$\n\n- Since $0,1$ and $2$ are *not* elements of $B,$ we write $0,1,2\\notin B.$\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$\\,$  $-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}\\in A$ and $0,1,2\\notin B$",
